VERDICT: FAIL -- VLANs cannot be added or deleted through the RESTCONF API, and PATCH of an interface's
         `enabled` leaf answers 204 but changes nothing; interface disable/enable by PUT on that leaf works
      interface disable, PUT enabled leaf false ...... PASS  (204; CLI "admin down", `shutdown`, log "user shutdown";
                                                              4050 port1.0.5 down; openconfig and ietf paths)
      interface enable, PUT enabled leaf true ........ PASS  (204; CLI "admin up running", log "user no shutdown")
      interface disable/enable, PATCH enabled leaf ... FAIL  (204 No Content, state and CLI unchanged)
      VLAN add .......................................  FAIL  (PUT ietf-interfaces vlan3999 -> 204, GET shows it;
                                                              show vlan brief has no VLAN 3999)
      VLAN delete .................................... FAIL  (DELETE of the real vlan3999 -> 403 access-denied,
                                                              ietf and openconfig paths; VLAN 3999 still ACTIVE)
FAIL CONDITION: the case's "add/delete vlans" through the API cannot be done. (1) Create: the write that is
         accepted (PUT .../ietf-interfaces:interfaces/interface=vlan3999) answers 204 No Content and the API then
         reads the object back, yet the device has no VLAN 3999 and no vlan3999 interface: a reported success
         with nothing created. No exposed model holds the VLAN list: GET openconfig-vlan:vlans, .../vlan=105
         and at-l2-topology:vlans all answer 404 "uri path not found". (2) Delete: a VLAN that really exists
         (created on the CLI, and visible to the API as type l3ipvlan) is refused 403 access-denied on both the
         ietf and openconfig paths, for user manager (privilege 15). (3) PATCH of the `enabled` leaf answers
         204 No Content and changes nothing, three times out of three. These are product failures, not the
         method: the same client, user, transport and leaf path change the port at once with PUT (proven on
         the CLI of both link ends), so a 204 that does nothing is the server's answer, not a bad request.
